1
00:00:03,960 --> 00:00:05,640
What color would you like to be?

2
00:00:05,640 --> 00:00:08,270
Well, I'd like to be green, but you know you always take it.

3
00:00:08,270 --> 00:00:09,680
That's not true.

4
00:00:09,680 --> 00:00:11,410
Any color's fine with me.

5
00:00:11,410 --> 00:00:15,820
Yeah, I could be a-a combination of blue and yellow.

6
00:00:16,430 --> 00:00:17,700
Blue and yellow make green.

7
00:00:17,700 --> 00:00:19,580
Well, then it's settled.

8
00:00:21,420 --> 00:00:22,670
Hi. Ready to go?

9
00:00:22,670 --> 00:00:24,520
Oh, good news, we ordered lunch,

10
00:00:24,520 --> 00:00:28,330
so we can all stay here and play Lord of the Rings Risk.

11
00:00:28,330 --> 00:00:31,160
Sheldon, we said that we would play games with you tonight.

12
00:00:31,160 --> 00:00:32,630
Oh, no, we'll still be playing it tonight--

13
00:00:32,630 --> 00:00:35,170
this game can easily take eight hours.

14
00:00:35,610 --> 00:00:37,080
Sweetie, you really thought I'd want to do this?

15
00:00:37,080 --> 00:00:38,460
- No. - Well, did you tell him that?

16
00:00:38,460 --> 00:00:41,920
- Yes. - Did you say it out loud with words?

17
00:00:41,920 --> 00:00:43,880
No.

18
00:00:44,350 --> 00:00:46,540
I don't want to spend the whole day playing a board game.

19
00:00:46,540 --> 00:00:48,380
Yeah, well, you may change your mind when you hear

20
00:00:48,380 --> 00:00:50,800
that this is the new expanded edition

21
00:00:50,800 --> 00:00:53,140
which contains a more complete map of Middle Earth,

22
00:00:53,140 --> 00:00:56,030
now including the Haradwaith Territories.

23
00:00:57,740 --> 00:01:00,430
I will literally race you to the car.

24
00:01:00,580 --> 00:01:02,690
No, no, no, come on, don't leave. Just try it.

25
00:01:02,690 --> 00:01:04,420
No. We're always doing what you guys want.

26
00:01:04,420 --> 00:01:07,230
Just once, it'd be nice if you did something we wanted.

27
00:01:07,230 --> 00:01:09,410
You want to be green?

28
00:01:11,610 --> 00:01:12,800
You know, they really have tried to

29
00:01:12,800 --> 00:01:14,630
like a lot of the same stuff we're into.

30
00:01:14,630 --> 00:01:16,980
Yeah, we do game nights and video game nights

31
00:01:16,980 --> 00:01:19,090
and we watch movies with director's commentary.

32
00:01:19,090 --> 00:01:19,930
Oh, my favorite--

33
00:01:19,930 --> 00:01:22,080
George Lucas can talk all the way through Star Wars,

34
00:01:22,080 --> 00:01:25,170
I say one word and I'm banished to the kitchen.

35
00:01:25,970 --> 00:01:28,650
Yeah, today Amy and I are deciding what we're all gonna do.

36
00:01:28,650 --> 00:01:30,600
You got it, you girls are in charge.

37
00:01:30,600 --> 00:01:32,730
- Thank you. - Sheldon?

38
00:01:33,210 --> 00:01:34,750
Fine.

39
00:01:34,750 --> 00:01:37,490
Now that we're not playing, you can be green.

40
00:01:37,490 --> 00:01:38,910
- Thank you. - And since you're green this time,

41
00:01:38,910 --> 00:01:41,040
I can be it next time.

42
00:01:41,350 --> 00:01:42,790
All right, let's see. What's something fun

43
00:01:42,790 --> 00:01:44,690
the guys would never take us to do?

44
00:01:44,690 --> 00:01:46,510
Oh, I know-- we could go horseback riding.

45
00:01:46,510 --> 00:01:47,800
I actually can't.

46
00:01:47,800 --> 00:01:51,620
My hips don't open wider than 22 degrees.

47
00:01:53,080 --> 00:01:56,070
I rode a very thin pony once.

48
00:01:56,070 --> 00:01:59,520
On the first bump... just popped right off.

49
00:02:01,060 --> 00:02:03,120
All right, well, what do you want to do?

50
00:02:03,120 --> 00:02:05,730
There's a craft and folk art museum on Wilshire.

51
00:02:05,730 --> 00:02:07,990
Well, that's Wilshire's problem.

52
00:02:09,450 --> 00:02:11,320
Come on, you know, there-there's got to be something fun

53
00:02:11,320 --> 00:02:12,970
we could do that the guys will hate.

54
00:02:12,970 --> 00:02:14,820
Hang on, why do we have to hate it?

55
00:02:14,820 --> 00:02:18,240
Three words: Doctor Who convention.

56
00:02:18,240 --> 00:02:20,010
I-I did not force you to go to that.

57
00:02:20,010 --> 00:02:22,160
You walked out of the house in a fez and bow tie.

58
00:02:22,160 --> 00:02:23,890
I went so you didn't get beat up.

59
00:02:24,450 --> 00:02:25,770
I wasn't gonna get beat up.

60
00:02:25,770 --> 00:02:28,760
You were, but somehow I held myself back.

61
00:02:29,290 --> 00:02:31,710
You know what you could make us do? Ice-skating.

62
00:02:31,710 --> 00:02:33,930
The cold air will trigger Leonard's asthma

63
00:02:33,930 --> 00:02:35,890
and it plays right into my well-known fear of

64
00:02:35,890 --> 00:02:38,960
getting flattened by a Zamboni.

65
00:02:39,480 --> 00:02:42,310
Now you're helping them find ways to make us miserable?

66
00:02:42,310 --> 00:02:43,200
Well, I'm sorry, Leonard,

67
00:02:43,200 --> 00:02:45,140
I'm a problem-solver, it's what I do.

68
00:02:46,350 --> 00:02:48,240
I actually can't go ice-skating.

69
00:02:48,240 --> 00:02:51,720
I have unnaturally brittle ankles.

70
00:02:51,720 --> 00:02:54,760
Is there any part of your body that's normal?

71
00:03:22,960 --> 00:03:25,200
Oh, my God, oh, my God, oh, my God.

72
00:03:25,200 --> 00:03:27,780
- Will you please relax? - I can't take it, dude.

73
00:03:27,780 --> 00:03:29,400
- You okay? - No, I'm not okay.

74
00:03:29,400 --> 00:03:31,830
I feel like I'm gonna jump out of my skin.

75
00:03:31,830 --> 00:03:34,820
I told you not to wax down there.

76
00:03:35,850 --> 00:03:38,550
It's itchy when it grows back.

77
00:03:39,630 --> 00:03:43,210
I'm worried about the New Horizons space probe.

78
00:03:43,210 --> 00:03:44,390
What's he talking about?

79
00:03:44,390 --> 00:03:46,820
Nine years ago he was part of a team

80
00:03:46,820 --> 00:03:49,630
that launched a spacecraft to collect data about Pluto.

81
00:03:49,630 --> 00:03:51,120
It's finally close enough,

82
00:03:51,120 --> 00:03:53,190
so this morning it turned itself on.

83
00:03:53,190 --> 00:03:54,090
We hope.

84
00:03:54,090 --> 00:03:56,520
The signal has to travel over three billion miles.

85
00:03:56,520 --> 00:03:59,430
So it's gonna be hours before we know if it even survived.

86
00:03:59,430 --> 00:04:00,800
Now we get to see him flip out because he's worried

87
00:04:00,800 --> 00:04:02,420
it was demolished by space ice.

88
00:04:02,420 --> 00:04:04,670
Space ice is no joke.

89
00:04:05,400 --> 00:04:08,240
I can't even watch Frozen anymore.

90
00:04:10,930 --> 00:04:13,770
Ooh, the philharmonic is playing Beethoven downtown.

91
00:04:13,770 --> 00:04:15,760
Yeah, well, before you say yes,

92
00:04:15,760 --> 00:04:19,610
it's not the movie about the big dog.

93
00:04:21,630 --> 00:04:24,540
How come we can't think of something we both want to do?

94
00:04:24,540 --> 00:04:26,490
Because you always pick what we do

95
00:04:26,490 --> 00:04:28,250
and I just go along with it.

96
00:04:28,250 --> 00:04:29,630
Ah, interesting--

97
00:04:29,630 --> 00:04:31,860
we're being accused of making you do things you don't like,

98
00:04:31,860 --> 00:04:34,520
and here you are, doing the same thing to poor Amy.

99
00:04:34,520 --> 00:04:37,620
You should point out the hypocrisy of that.

100
00:04:40,830 --> 00:04:42,500
That's what I was doing.

101
00:04:43,310 --> 00:04:45,290
Oh, that wasn't clear.

102
00:04:45,770 --> 00:04:47,740
Try it again, but this time drive it home

103
00:04:47,740 --> 00:04:50,760
with, "How do you like them apples, missy?"

104
00:04:51,640 --> 00:04:53,330
All right, keep thinking.

105
00:04:53,330 --> 00:04:55,300
You're making it too complicated.

106
00:04:55,300 --> 00:04:56,800
Why not stick to the basics?

107
00:04:56,800 --> 00:04:58,200
Go shopping for clothes

108
00:04:58,200 --> 00:05:00,230
while Leonard and I sit in those uncomfortable chairs

109
00:05:00,230 --> 00:05:01,800
and hold your purses, hmm?

110
00:05:01,800 --> 00:05:03,430
I know I'd hate that.

111
00:05:03,430 --> 00:05:04,640
Leonard?

112
00:05:04,640 --> 00:05:07,210
Well, yeah.

113
00:05:07,210 --> 00:05:09,780
Well, then, it's settled?

114
00:05:09,780 --> 00:05:11,930
What do you say? Sounds kind of perfect.

115
00:05:11,930 --> 00:05:16,000
It does-- somehow he managed to take all the fun out of it.

116
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:19,370
Well, once again, it's what I do.

117
00:05:21,440 --> 00:05:23,700
Oh, another two hours to go.

118
00:05:23,700 --> 00:05:25,360
The wait is killing me.

119
00:05:25,360 --> 00:05:26,530
I know. I get it.

120
00:05:26,530 --> 00:05:29,520
When I was in the Soyuz capsule returning from the space station,

121
00:05:29,520 --> 00:05:31,640
plummeting toward Earth at 17,000 miles per hour...

122
00:05:31,640 --> 00:05:32,540
Before you finish,

123
00:05:32,540 --> 00:05:35,490
is this a story about patience and waiting

124
00:05:35,490 --> 00:05:39,300
or just another reminder that you went to space?

125
00:05:40,580 --> 00:05:42,840
A story can do two things.

126
00:05:43,150 --> 00:05:45,550
Ugh, I feel like I'm gonna have a heart attack.

127
00:05:45,550 --> 00:05:46,860
You work in pharmaceuticals--

128
00:05:46,860 --> 00:05:49,500
don't you have anything you can give him?

129
00:05:49,850 --> 00:05:53,070
All I have is our new urine flow drug.

130
00:05:53,740 --> 00:05:55,120
Won't help with his anxiety,

131
00:05:55,120 --> 00:05:56,620
but it's so strong, when he pees

132
00:05:56,620 --> 00:06:00,040
he'll fly around the room like he's got a jet pack.

133
00:06:01,060 --> 00:06:03,700
I can't stop thinking about it.

134
00:06:03,700 --> 00:06:06,230
You know, worrying won't have any effect on what happens.

135
00:06:06,230 --> 00:06:07,280
I know.

136
00:06:07,280 --> 00:06:09,910
Maybe you need to do something more productive.

137
00:06:10,940 --> 00:06:12,580
Okay.

138
00:06:13,790 --> 00:06:16,780
If I make this shot

139
00:06:16,780 --> 00:06:18,470
in the trash can,

140
00:06:18,750 --> 00:06:20,390
the probe will have arrived

141
00:06:20,390 --> 00:06:23,370
in perfect working order.

142
00:06:23,370 --> 00:06:25,430
So, in addition to being crazy, you're resting the fate

143
00:06:25,430 --> 00:06:28,800
of the mission on your athletic prowess.

144
00:06:29,250 --> 00:06:29,990
Yes.

145
00:06:29,990 --> 00:06:33,160
The man who crashed his stationary bike.

146
00:06:33,990 --> 00:06:36,220
I didn't crash it, okay?

147
00:06:36,220 --> 00:06:37,890
My playlist was too up-tempo,

148
00:06:37,890 --> 00:06:41,000
I got light-headed and I fell off.

149
00:06:41,790 --> 00:06:43,520
Okay.

150
00:06:44,160 --> 00:06:45,740
It all...

151
00:06:45,740 --> 00:06:47,370
comes down...

152
00:06:48,610 --> 00:06:50,730
to this.

153
00:06:52,830 --> 00:06:55,000
You happy? Now you can relax.

154
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:56,270
What kind of scientist are you?

155
00:06:56,270 --> 00:06:58,950
Everyone knows you got to make two out of three.

156
00:07:06,410 --> 00:07:08,260
This isn't so bad.

157
00:07:09,100 --> 00:07:10,660
That's easy for you to say.

158
00:07:10,660 --> 00:07:14,110
Your chair's not facing the lingerie section.

159
00:07:15,200 --> 00:07:18,100
Boy, that's a lot of panties.

160
00:07:18,430 --> 00:07:21,150
You guys comfy? This might take a while.

161
00:07:21,150 --> 00:07:22,720
You know, I don't understand why women insist

162
00:07:22,720 --> 00:07:25,430
on making a big production out of buying clothes.

163
00:07:25,430 --> 00:07:27,050
No, you're right, we should do what you do.

164
00:07:27,050 --> 00:07:31,270
Have our mom send us pants from the Walmart in Houston.

165
00:07:31,930 --> 00:07:33,190
They have a man there

166
00:07:33,190 --> 00:07:36,310
who understands my personal style.

167
00:07:36,310 --> 00:07:37,940
Bye.

168
00:07:37,940 --> 00:07:40,210
Uh, I've got some bad news.

169
00:07:40,210 --> 00:07:41,990
There's no cell service in here.

170
00:07:43,780 --> 00:07:46,040
Well, that's all right.

171
00:07:46,040 --> 00:07:47,340
There were plenty of ways to pass the time

172
00:07:47,340 --> 00:07:49,570
before smart phones were invented.

173
00:07:49,570 --> 00:07:50,810
That's true.

174
00:07:51,230 --> 00:07:52,960
I'll look them up.

175
00:07:55,720 --> 00:07:58,490
Son of a biscuit.

176
00:07:58,950 --> 00:08:00,020
Sheldon, it's fine.

177
00:08:00,020 --> 00:08:01,350
No, it's not fine.

178
00:08:01,350 --> 00:08:04,700
What kind of store in the 21st century doesn't at least have Wi-Fi?

179
00:08:04,700 --> 00:08:06,110
I'm going to call their corporate office...

180
00:08:06,110 --> 00:08:08,340
Son of a biscuit!

181
00:08:12,790 --> 00:08:17,040
Let's see, my armies are going to attack

182
00:08:17,040 --> 00:08:19,480
the Shire from Buckland.

183
00:08:19,480 --> 00:08:22,600
And I roll...

184
00:08:26,360 --> 00:08:28,570
a five and a three.

185
00:08:28,570 --> 00:08:29,680
Okay.

186
00:08:29,680 --> 00:08:33,440
And to defend, I roll...

187
00:08:34,320 --> 00:08:36,500
two sixes. I win.

188
00:08:37,220 --> 00:08:40,330
Boy, double-sixes again.

189
00:08:40,330 --> 00:08:41,570
You know... if I hadn't seen it with my own eyes,

190
00:08:41,570 --> 00:08:44,220
I wouldn't believe it.

191
00:08:44,970 --> 00:08:47,000
I wonder how long we're gonna be stuck here.

192
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:48,860
I don't know, but the girls do a lot for us.

193
00:08:48,860 --> 00:08:50,230
It's the least we can do.

194
00:08:50,230 --> 00:08:51,370
Oh, that's true.

195
00:08:51,370 --> 00:08:54,690
I suppose it's only fair we make compromises.

196
00:08:54,690 --> 00:08:56,990
Look at you, being all mature.

197
00:08:57,490 --> 00:09:00,250
I don't know why you're so surprised.

198
00:09:00,250 --> 00:09:01,940
If there's one thing I know about after living with you

199
00:09:01,940 --> 00:09:05,460
for so many years, it's how to compromise.

200
00:09:05,970 --> 00:09:07,740
I-I'm sorry?

201
00:09:07,740 --> 00:09:10,290
You make compromises for me?

202
00:09:11,410 --> 00:09:13,680
All the time.

203
00:09:14,530 --> 00:09:16,890
On Earth?

204
00:09:18,760 --> 00:09:21,370
In our lives?

205
00:09:21,930 --> 00:09:24,700
That-That we're living?

206
00:09:24,700 --> 00:09:26,620
Oh, yes. I...

207
00:09:26,620 --> 00:09:30,110
Just yesterday, you had a-a big piece of lettuce

208
00:09:30,110 --> 00:09:31,620
stuck in your teeth at lunch.

209
00:09:31,620 --> 00:09:33,530
Did I say anything?

210
00:09:33,530 --> 00:09:35,360
No.

211
00:09:36,390 --> 00:09:38,210
I compromised

212
00:09:38,210 --> 00:09:40,640
and kept my mouth shut.

213
00:09:40,640 --> 00:09:44,220
Like you should've, because everyone was laughing at you.

214
00:09:45,330 --> 00:09:46,790
That is not a compromise.

215
00:09:46,790 --> 00:09:48,970
A compromise is me driving you everywhere

216
00:09:48,970 --> 00:09:50,920
because you refuse to learn how.

217
00:09:50,920 --> 00:09:53,140
Oh, I learned how. Amy taught me.

218
00:09:53,140 --> 00:09:55,030
What? Then-Then why don't you do it?

219
00:09:55,030 --> 00:09:58,070
Uh, well, it's scary.

220
00:09:59,050 --> 00:10:02,660
And sometimes I get the pedals mixed up.

221
00:10:03,420 --> 00:10:06,420
But, more importantly, driving me to work

222
00:10:06,420 --> 00:10:09,860
is one of the things that gives your life purpose.

223
00:10:10,900 --> 00:10:12,870
I can't take that away from you,

224
00:10:12,870 --> 00:10:16,030
so... what do I do?

225
00:10:17,230 --> 00:10:20,000
Oh, come on, I'm practically feeding you the answer.

226
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:22,750
I compromise.

227
00:10:24,870 --> 00:10:28,080
- Hey, uh, thanks for keeping me company. - I'm happy to.

228
00:10:28,080 --> 00:10:30,790
I think getting out of the apartment will do you good.

229
00:10:30,790 --> 00:10:33,610
So... where we headed?

230
00:10:33,610 --> 00:10:36,450
If it's okay with you, I'd like to go to temple.

231
00:10:37,070 --> 00:10:40,410
Buddy, trust me, you don't want to convert to Judaism.

232
00:10:41,280 --> 00:10:43,020
I mean, I know I make it look cool,

233
00:10:43,020 --> 00:10:47,720
but... it's not all briskets and dreidels.

234
00:10:48,130 --> 00:10:50,590
I meant a Hindu temple.

235
00:10:50,590 --> 00:10:53,530
Oh. Okay.

236
00:10:53,530 --> 00:10:55,820
It's not like Indiana Jones and the Temple of Doom, right?

237
00:10:55,820 --> 00:10:59,250
Some bald guy with horns isn't gonna rip my heart out.

238
00:10:59,250 --> 00:11:02,250
Dude, that movie's an imperialist fantasy

239
00:11:02,250 --> 00:11:05,540
that makes the followers of a beautiful and peaceful religion

240
00:11:05,540 --> 00:11:07,930
look like a bunch of bloodthirsty barbarians.

241
00:11:07,930 --> 00:11:08,910
You love that movie.

242
00:11:08,910 --> 00:11:11,620
Yeah, it's pretty great.

243
00:11:14,690 --> 00:11:17,590
I'm surprised to see you suddenly get religious.

244
00:11:17,590 --> 00:11:18,560
Why?

245
00:11:18,560 --> 00:11:21,080
Well, because I've known you for ten years

246
00:11:21,080 --> 00:11:22,840
and you've never gone to temple,

247
00:11:22,840 --> 00:11:25,310
you never talked about believing in God,

248
00:11:25,310 --> 00:11:28,940
and last Diwali I watched you eat two pounds of sacred cow

249
00:11:28,940 --> 00:11:31,980
at a Brazilian steak house.

250
00:11:31,980 --> 00:11:35,160
Religion is a very personal thing.

251
00:11:35,160 --> 00:11:37,400
I do go to temple-- I just... I don't talk about it.

252
00:11:37,400 --> 00:11:39,470
Yeah, but you're a scientist.

253
00:11:39,470 --> 00:11:40,020
So?

254
00:11:40,020 --> 00:11:42,180
So, as a scientist, you believe the way to understand the universe

255
00:11:42,180 --> 00:11:43,750
is through facts and evidence,

256
00:11:43,750 --> 00:11:45,360
and now you're counting on some

257
00:11:45,360 --> 00:11:48,680
blue chick with a hundred arms to help you?

258
00:11:48,680 --> 00:11:50,260
That is so offensive.

259
00:11:50,260 --> 00:11:52,450
Does everything you know about Hinduism come from Indiana Jones?

260
00:11:52,450 --> 00:11:56,820
No. There's also Apu from The Simpsons.

261
00:11:56,820 --> 00:12:00,670
Well, lots of scientists believe in God.

262
00:12:00,670 --> 00:12:03,130
Okay? Newton, uh, Faraday,

263
00:12:03,130 --> 00:12:05,100
uh, Pascal-- all were believers.

264
00:12:05,100 --> 00:12:06,770
Even Einstein was famous for

265
00:12:06,770 --> 00:12:08,590
attacking quantum theory on the grounds that

266
00:12:08,590 --> 00:12:11,140
"God does not play dice with the universe."

267
00:12:11,140 --> 00:12:12,720
Well, of course he believed in God--

268
00:12:12,720 --> 00:12:15,460
he slept with Marilyn Monroe.

269
00:12:15,460 --> 00:12:16,990
Actually there's no proof of that.

270
00:12:16,990 --> 00:12:20,320
You believe in your religion, I'll believe in mine.

271
00:12:22,970 --> 00:12:24,560
How's it going in there?

272
00:12:25,940 --> 00:12:28,580
Not really a great outfit for work,

273
00:12:28,580 --> 00:12:32,330
unless something opens up in the hookers and whores division.

274
00:12:33,090 --> 00:12:35,720
- Hey, can I ask you something? - Sure.

275
00:12:35,720 --> 00:12:38,250
Do I really force you to do things you don't want to?

276
00:12:38,250 --> 00:12:40,140
Yeah, but it's okay.

277
00:12:40,140 --> 00:12:41,700
How is it okay?

278
00:12:41,700 --> 00:12:43,750
I promised myself, if I ever got friends,

279
00:12:43,750 --> 00:12:45,970
I'd do whatever they said.

280
00:12:46,760 --> 00:12:50,350
Really, I'm lucky you found me before a cult did.

281
00:12:51,210 --> 00:12:53,120
Well, you know, that was a long time ago.

282
00:12:53,120 --> 00:12:54,830
You're a different woman now.

283
00:12:54,830 --> 00:12:57,530
You're smart, you've got great friends,

284
00:12:57,530 --> 00:12:59,230
you've got a boyfriend, you're pretty,

285
00:12:59,230 --> 00:13:02,150
you have zero fashion sense, but...

286
00:13:02,150 --> 00:13:05,200
Anyway, tonight we're gonna do whatever you want.

287
00:13:05,200 --> 00:13:06,790
- Really? - Absolutely.

288
00:13:06,790 --> 00:13:09,140
You name it, we're doing it.

289
00:13:09,670 --> 00:13:12,680
Basket weaving at the craft museum.

290
00:13:13,870 --> 00:13:16,580
Well, you named it.

291
00:13:19,940 --> 00:13:21,850
Oh, here's another one.

292
00:13:21,850 --> 00:13:25,970
I wish that the apple pancake mix was on the top shelf

293
00:13:25,970 --> 00:13:27,740
because it starts with an "A,"

294
00:13:27,740 --> 00:13:29,340
but I don't put it there

295
00:13:29,340 --> 00:13:31,520
because I don't want you breaking one of your little legs

296
00:13:31,520 --> 00:13:33,930
when you're supposed to be making my breakfast.

297
00:13:36,270 --> 00:13:39,990
Is it my turn to talk about the compromises I make?

298
00:13:40,340 --> 00:13:43,180
I wasn't done, but go ahead.

299
00:13:44,110 --> 00:13:46,720
He said, compromising.

300
00:13:47,730 --> 00:13:49,340
Because of you, I'm not allowed

301
00:13:49,340 --> 00:13:51,610
to adjust the temperature in my own home.

302
00:13:51,610 --> 00:13:52,840
I'm not allowed to whistle.

303
00:13:52,840 --> 00:13:55,100
I don't wear shoes that might squeak.

304
00:13:55,100 --> 00:13:58,920
Well, you're a physicist, not a circus clown.

305
00:13:59,340 --> 00:14:02,670
Sheldon, do you realize I don't live with the woman I love

306
00:14:02,670 --> 00:14:04,500
because of you?

307
00:14:04,500 --> 00:14:06,740
No other reason. Just you.

308
00:14:06,740 --> 00:14:08,120
- Is that true? - Yes, it's true.

309
00:14:08,120 --> 00:14:10,150
The last time I brought it up, you had an emotional breakdown

310
00:14:10,150 --> 00:14:12,930
and got on a train and ran away.

311
00:14:12,930 --> 00:14:14,830
Well, given my history on the subject,

312
00:14:14,830 --> 00:14:17,830
it seems a little reckless to bring it up now.

313
00:14:19,300 --> 00:14:22,090
You have no idea how much you inconvenience

314
00:14:22,090 --> 00:14:23,810
the lives of everyone around you.

315
00:14:23,810 --> 00:14:25,460
It's exhausting!

316
00:14:25,460 --> 00:14:26,990
You know what?

317
00:14:27,470 --> 00:14:29,120
You think you're so tolerant,

318
00:14:29,120 --> 00:14:31,080
but the truth is...

319
00:14:31,420 --> 00:14:33,230
you're mean to me a lot.

320
00:14:33,230 --> 00:14:36,370
Yeah, you think that I don't notice all those sarcastic comments

321
00:14:36,370 --> 00:14:38,590
and those eye rolls, but I do. Hmm?

322
00:14:38,590 --> 00:14:41,940
I have excellent peripheral vision.

323
00:14:42,490 --> 00:14:45,930
On a good day, I can see my ears.

324
00:14:47,880 --> 00:14:50,250
Sheldon, I-I'm sorry if I hurt your feelings.

325
00:14:50,250 --> 00:14:52,570
I've been holding a lot of this in for a while.

326
00:14:52,570 --> 00:14:53,770
Well, I'm sorry, too.

327
00:14:53,770 --> 00:14:55,650
And if you want to live with Penny,

328
00:14:55,650 --> 00:14:57,680
then I think you should.

329
00:14:57,680 --> 00:14:59,200
- Do you mean that? - Yes.

330
00:14:59,200 --> 00:15:01,420
Yeah, just put on your squeaky shoes and...

331
00:15:01,420 --> 00:15:04,440
your way out of my life.

332
00:15:06,590 --> 00:15:08,180
Come on, don't get upset.

333
00:15:09,090 --> 00:15:11,000
I'm not upset.

334
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:15,560
I'm just imagining a world without my best friend in it.

335
00:15:16,840 --> 00:15:17,900
Sheldon.

336
00:15:17,900 --> 00:15:19,380
It's okay.

337
00:15:19,700 --> 00:15:22,080
I-I'm not leaving your world.

338
00:15:22,080 --> 00:15:24,460
I'm just talking about living across the hall.

339
00:15:24,460 --> 00:15:25,970
I understand.

340
00:15:25,970 --> 00:15:28,660
Either way, I want you to know that...

341
00:15:28,660 --> 00:15:30,930
I'm aware of how difficult I can be.

342
00:15:30,930 --> 00:15:33,040
So I just want to say...

343
00:15:33,990 --> 00:15:36,910
thank you for putting up with me.

344
00:15:37,430 --> 00:15:39,180
Buddy...

345
00:15:42,900 --> 00:15:44,130
How are you guys getting alo...?

346
00:15:44,130 --> 00:15:47,270
What? Why are there tears?

347
00:15:47,270 --> 00:15:48,170
Everything's fine.

348
00:15:48,170 --> 00:15:50,740
We just started talking about living arrangements.

349
00:15:50,740 --> 00:15:54,470
Are you crazy? You know he's a flight risk.

350
00:15:55,480 --> 00:15:58,300
That's exactly what I told him.

351
00:15:58,300 --> 00:16:00,700
Sheldon, we know this is a sensitive subject,

352
00:16:00,700 --> 00:16:03,350
and Leonard's not gonna move out until you're ready.

353
00:16:05,060 --> 00:16:08,390
Well, what if you did it gradually?

354
00:16:09,330 --> 00:16:11,060
All right. Well...

355
00:16:11,060 --> 00:16:14,280
how about we start with two nights a week

356
00:16:14,280 --> 00:16:16,030
I live with Penny?

357
00:16:16,030 --> 00:16:20,010
How about one night and...

358
00:16:20,010 --> 00:16:22,250
I let you whistle?

359
00:16:26,280 --> 00:16:27,070
Okay.

360
00:16:27,070 --> 00:16:29,620
When I'm not home.

361
00:16:31,010 --> 00:16:33,110
You got it.

362
00:16:34,170 --> 00:16:35,450
There we go,

363
00:16:35,450 --> 00:16:38,300
compromising again.

364
00:16:38,730 --> 00:16:40,750
We really are the best.

365
00:16:48,890 --> 00:16:50,160
Here we go,

366
00:16:50,160 --> 00:16:51,920
my first Hindu temple.

367
00:16:51,920 --> 00:16:53,780
You see behind the fountain,

368
00:16:53,780 --> 00:16:55,920
that tower that looks like a pyramid?

369
00:16:55,920 --> 00:16:57,280
It's called a Sikhara.

370
00:16:57,280 --> 00:17:01,490
It symbolizes the-the connection between the human and the divine.

371
00:17:02,260 --> 00:17:04,930
I always thought it was mini golf.

372
00:17:05,590 --> 00:17:07,690
- All right. Shall we? - Yeah.

373
00:17:07,690 --> 00:17:11,160
Just, uh, is there anything I should know

374
00:17:11,160 --> 00:17:13,160
- before I go in? - Like what?

375
00:17:13,160 --> 00:17:16,430
Like am I dressed okay?

376
00:17:16,430 --> 00:17:18,230
Really? So every other place you've been,

377
00:17:18,230 --> 00:17:20,940
you thought this was fine?

378
00:17:22,190 --> 00:17:23,560
I know you're under a lot of pressure,

379
00:17:23,560 --> 00:17:25,830
so I'm gonna let that pass.

380
00:17:25,830 --> 00:17:26,870
Sorry. You're right.

381
00:17:26,870 --> 00:17:28,500
I'm so stressed.

382
00:17:28,910 --> 00:17:32,160
But you know what, whenever I walk into that temple

383
00:17:32,160 --> 00:17:35,530
I realize that whatever happens, it's okay.

384
00:17:35,530 --> 00:17:37,110
We're all part of an immense pattern,

385
00:17:37,110 --> 00:17:39,670
and though we can't understand it, we can be happy to know

386
00:17:39,670 --> 00:17:42,730
that it's-it's working its will through us.

387
00:17:43,440 --> 00:17:45,020
That's nice.

388
00:17:45,020 --> 00:17:47,880
Whether you call it God or the universe or the self,

389
00:17:47,880 --> 00:17:50,630
we're all interconnected, and that's just a beautiful...

390
00:17:51,100 --> 00:17:54,870
Son of a bitch! That guy just dinged my car!

391
00:17:56,980 --> 00:17:58,030
S-Seriously?

392
00:17:58,030 --> 00:17:59,920
Y-You were just gonna drive away?

393
00:17:59,920 --> 00:18:02,550
Like my life isn't hard enough right now.

394
00:18:02,550 --> 00:18:05,220
A space probe might be destroyed,

395
00:18:05,220 --> 00:18:07,300
my parents are going through an awful divorce,

396
00:18:07,300 --> 00:18:11,620
the guy who cuts my dog's hair just gave her bangs!

397
00:18:11,620 --> 00:18:13,400
- Raj. - You saw her.

398
00:18:13,400 --> 00:18:16,870
She looks like Jim Carrey from Dumb and Dumber!

399
00:18:17,450 --> 00:18:19,080
Raj, you just got a text.

400
00:18:19,080 --> 00:18:21,890
The probe turned on. It's fine.

401
00:18:23,250 --> 00:18:25,190
Oh, good.

402
00:18:26,260 --> 00:18:28,570
Namaste, Grandpa.

403
00:18:36,880 --> 00:18:38,390
I thought this was gonna be boring,

404
00:18:38,390 --> 00:18:40,250
but it's actually kind of fun.

405
00:18:40,250 --> 00:18:43,370
Don't tell Amy that. We'll be here every Sunday.

406
00:18:44,160 --> 00:18:47,050
Sheldon, that really is an excellent basket.

407
00:18:47,050 --> 00:18:48,350
It's not a basket.

408
00:18:48,350 --> 00:18:52,610
It's a soldier's helmet from 16th century China.

409
00:18:55,560 --> 00:18:57,210
Very nice.

410
00:18:57,570 --> 00:18:59,110
Yeah, it looks great.

411
00:19:02,680 --> 00:19:04,540
I saw that.

412
00:19:11,860 --> 00:19:14,360
Well, roomie, it's only one night a week,

413
00:19:14,360 --> 00:19:15,390
but it's a start.

414
00:19:15,390 --> 00:19:17,390
I know. I'm really proud of Sheldon.

415
00:19:17,390 --> 00:19:19,070
Yeah, I'm proud of him, too.

416
00:19:19,070 --> 00:19:20,780
Can you keep it down?

417
00:19:20,780 --> 00:19:23,910
Some of us are trying to sleep out here.

